    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT & PRINCIPLE:\nLassaigne's test for elemental nitrogen detection in organic analysis.\n\n2. GIVEN INFORMATION / DATA:\nSodium fusion extract containing $NaCN$ boiled with $FeSO_4$ and acidified with concentrated $HCl$ / $FeCl_3$.\n\n3. FORMULA / EQUATION:\n$6NaCN + FeSO_4 \\rightarrow Na_4[Fe(CN)_6] + Na_2SO_4$; $3Na_4[Fe(CN)_6] + 4FeCl_3 \\rightarrow Fe_4[Fe(CN)_6]_3\\downarrow + 12NaCl$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP REASONING & CALCULATION:\n1. During sodium fusion, organic nitrogen and carbon fuse with sodium to form ionic sodium cyanide ($NaCN$).\n2. The extract is boiled with ferrous sulfate ($FeSO_4$), forming sodium hexacyanidoferrate(II): $6NaCN + FeSO_4 \\rightarrow Na_4[Fe(CN)_6] + Na_2SO_4$.\n3. Some $Fe^{2+}$ ions are oxidized to $Fe^{3+}$ by atmospheric oxygen or added $FeCl_3$.\n4. Acidification with concentrated $HCl$ dissolves ferrous hydroxide and allows $Fe^{3+}$ ions to react with hexacyanidoferrate(II) ions.\n5. This produces a colloidal Prussian blue precipitate of iron(III) hexacyanidoferrate(II), ferric ferrocyanide: $Fe_4[Fe(CN)_6]_3$.\n6. This intense blue coloration confirms the presence of nitrogen.\n\n5. FINAL CONCLUSION:\n$\\text{Fe}_4[\\text{Fe}(\\text{CN})_6]_3$\n\n6. OPTION ANALYSIS & COMMON TRAPS:\n$Fe_3[Fe(CN)_6]_2$ is Turnbull's blue (ferrous ferricyanide); $Na_4[Fe(CN)_5NOS]$ is the purple nitroprusside sulfur complex.",
